It was an honor to give a workshop talk at ICML. The core idea is that turning messy real-world outcomes into reliable, scalable reward signals enables training models where foundation models alone struggle. This approach also influences startup selection. For example, periodic Labs grades models with a physics fit against real measurements from its autonomous lab, pushing capability ceilings higher. Applied Compute audits large language models across many criteria before reinforcement learning, then trains open models to reach state-of-the-art on legal benchmarks. Elorian is developing verifiers that give models visual reasoning abilities, teaching them to see structure, where current frontier models fail at counting more than ten objects.

Coding agents started working because code shipped with free verifiers like compilers and type checkers. Reinforcement learning on these verifiers scaled with compute, boosting capabilities. The next unicorns will be startups building verifiers for more challenging domains.

@shaneguML also co-organized the first 'RL from World Feedback' workshop at ICML, featuring experts from OpenAI, Stanford, Princeton, UW, and others, highlighting the importance of learning from real-world signals.
